{
  "slug": "d-flip-flop",
  "title": "The D Flip-Flop: From Level to Edge",
  "excerpt": "The storage element every register and counter is actually built from,  and the reason it samples a clock edge instead of a level.",
  "authorName": "Lance Dy Chua",
  "status": "published",
  "publishedAt": "2026-07-30T09:30:00Z",
  "tags": [
    "sequential",
    "memory"
  ],
  "sectionSlug": "sequential-logic",
  "position": 40,
  "body": [
    {
      "id": "h-intro",
      "type": "heading",
      "level": 1,
      "text": "The D Flip-Flop: From Level to Edge"
    },
    {
      "id": "p-1",
      "type": "paragraph",
      "content": [
        {
          "text": "A D flip-flop looks, from the outside, almost identical to the gated D latch from the last tutorial: one data input D, one clock input CLK, and outputs Q and Q̄. The behavior is completely different, though, and the difference is exactly the fix the last tutorial promised. Instead of being transparent for an entire clock phase, a D flip-flop only ever looks at D for one infinitesimally brief instant per cycle: the moment CLK transitions from low to high, the "
        },
        {
          "text": "rising edge",
          "marks": [
            {
              "kind": "bold"
            }
          ]
        },
        {
          "text": ". Every other instant, no matter what D is doing, Q simply holds."
        }
      ]
    },
    {
      "id": "p-2",
      "type": "paragraph",
      "content": [
        {
          "text": "This is the difference between level-sensitive and edge-sensitive logic, and it's worth being precise about the wording: a level-sensitive latch cares about a signal's "
        },
        {
          "text": "value",
          "marks": [
            {
              "kind": "italic"
            }
          ]
        },
        {
          "text": " over some stretch of time (\"while E is high\"). An edge-sensitive flip-flop cares about a signal's "
        },
        {
          "text": "transition",
          "marks": [
            {
              "kind": "italic"
            }
          ]
        },
        {
          "text": " at one specific moment (\"the instant CLK goes from 0 to 1\"), and is completely blind to it the rest of the time. The circuit below is built exactly the way real hardware builds one: two gated D latches in series (a master and a slave), with the master's enable wired to CLK inverted and the slave's enable wired to CLK directly. Fifteen gates total, and every one of them is a NAND or a NOT you already know."
        }
      ]
    },
    {
      "id": "h-sampling",
      "type": "heading",
      "level": 2,
      "text": "Sampling Once Per Tick"
    },
    {
      "id": "p-3",
      "type": "paragraph",
      "content": [
        {
          "text": "Toggle D as many times as you like,  nothing happens to Q, because nothing you're touching is a clock edge. Now toggle CLK from 0 to 1 and back. Watch the master latch (the left half) freeze the instant CLK rises, right as the slave latch (the right half) opens up and copies whatever the master was holding out to Q. That handoff, master closing exactly as slave opens, is the entire mechanism, laid out in gates instead of asserted as a black box."
        }
      ]
    },
